        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'MODULE 2 IN PERSPECTIVE\n\nONE GOVERNING QUANTITY: THE CYCLE TIME\n\n  required rate -> T_c -> service time T_s\n  -> every station assignment must fit T_s\n  -> the number of stations follows\n  -> the feeder rates follow\n  -> the buffer sizes follow\n\nONE RECURRING LESSON: QUALITY AND RATE ARE COUPLED\n\n  MANUAL line   : worker meets a defective part,\n                  SETS IT ASIDE, carries on.\n                  Quality problem only.\n  AUTOMATED line: machine meets a defective part,\n                  JAMS and STOPS.\n                  Quality problem AND rate problem.\n\n  -> part defect rate is a PRODUCTION RATE PARAMETER,\n     not only a customer-facing one\n  -> which is WHY design for assembly matters far more\n     for automated systems than for manual ones\n\nDIAGNOSING AN UNDERPERFORMING MACHINE:\n\n  1. compare IDEAL cycle time with ACTUAL\n     ideal met between stoppages? -> not a speed problem\n  2. trace the STOPPAGES to their stations\n  3. trace those stations to their PARTS\n  4. examine DEFECT RATE and FEEDER reliability\n\n  The investigation usually ends in the DESIGN OFFICE,\n  not on the machine.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.15 and Ch.17, pp.392-496 (4th ed.).' },
