        dryRun: {
          input: 'A part has an internal cooling channel that follows a curved path. Which route?',
          steps: [
            'A curved internal channel cannot be drilled, since drilling produces straight holes',
            'Machining it would require the part to be split along the channel and rejoined, adding a joint and a leak path',
            'Additive manufacturing forms the channel as the layers pass through it, with no access required',
            'The channel surface finish will be poor, but for a cooling channel that may be acceptable or even beneficial',
          ],
          result: 'Additive manufacturing, and this is one of its clearest industrial applications: conformal cooling channels in injection moulding tools, which follow the contour of the cavity and cannot be produced by drilling at all.',
        },
        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'ADDITIVE vs SUBTRACTIVE\n\n  MACHINING (subtractive)\n    limited by TOOL ACCESS\n    - a feature the tool cannot REACH cannot be made\n    - internal cavities require the part to be SPLIT\n    + excellent SURFACE FINISH and ACCURACY\n    + full range of engineering materials, KNOWN properties\n    + very FAST for large volumes\n    - material removed becomes SWARF\n\n  ADDITIVE\n    material placed WHERE IT IS WANTED\n    + internal features and UNDERCUTS: no access problem\n    + geometric complexity FREE\n    + high material UTILISATION\n    - poorer surface finish and accuracy\n    - limited materials; properties often ANISOTROPIC\n    - SLOW for large volumes\n\nTHE CLEAREST INDUSTRIAL CASE - CONFORMAL COOLING:\n\n  an injection mould needs a cooling channel following\n  the CONTOUR of the cavity.\n\n  drilling produces STRAIGHT holes only.\n  machining it would mean SPLITTING the tool along the\n  channel and rejoining it - adding a joint and a LEAK\n  PATH at pressure.\n  AM forms the curved channel as the layers pass through\n  it. No access required at all.\n\n  The channel surface finish is poor - which for a\n  cooling channel is acceptable, and the turbulence may\n  even help.\n\nTHEY ARE COMPLEMENTARY, NOT COMPETING. That is the\nbasis of HYBRID MANUFACTURING.\n\nSOURCE: TB4 Ch.1, pp.9-16 (1st ed.).' },
        mistake: 'Students frame the two as competing, with one expected to replace the other. They are limited by different things, and the most common industrial outcome is a part built additively and then machined where the tolerances demand it.',
