        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'LIMITATIONS OF ADDITIVE MANUFACTURING\n\n  LOW BUILD RATE\n    cost per part does NOT fall with quantity the way\n    conventional processes allow\n    -> above a BREAK-EVEN QUANTITY, every conventional\n       route is cheaper\n\n  ANISOTROPY\n    WEAKER ACROSS the layers than within them\n    -> a load applied across the layers tends to\n       SEPARATE them - the weakest mode\n    -> orient so the PRINCIPAL LOAD lies WITHIN the\n       layer plane\n    -> the SAME bracket built the other way round may\n       fail at a FRACTION of the load - and LOOK\n       IDENTICAL\n    -> BUILD ORIENTATION MUST BE ON THE DRAWING, not\n       left to whoever sets up the machine\n\n  SURFACE FINISH\n    poorer than machining; functional surfaces need\n    post-processing\n\n  MATERIAL RANGE\n    growing, but far narrower than conventional, and\n    properties less well characterised\n\n  PART SIZE\n    limited by the machine BUILD VOLUME\n\nANISOTROPY IS THE MOST OVERLOOKED, precisely BECAUSE\nthe part looks the same whichever way it was built.\n\nThese are DESIGN CONSTRAINTS, not temporary\nshortcomings to be waited out.\n\nSOURCE: TB4 Ch.1, pp.9-16 and Ch.12, pp.317-340 (1st ed.).' },
